  "summary": "A jet pump has no moving parts. A motive fluid (water, steam or air) under pressure accelerates through a converging nozzle into a high-velocity jet. The jet's low static pressure and its momentum drag the surrounding suction fluid into the mixing throat, where the two streams exchange momentum and mix; the diffuser then slows the mixture and converts velocity back into pressure. Efficiency is modest, but the device is simple, robust, self-priming and handles slurries, corrosives and gases.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Kinetic – jet (ejector/eductor)",
    "configuration": "Liquid-motive eductor, single nozzle"
  },
